('021ead88-df0d-56d4-98a0-a7066277a1fe', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', 'c7d1948a-561e-56c0-8776-090012819a77', 'a-level', '29.4', '9702 coursebook 29.4 · Mass defect and binding energy', 45, 45, 'Fusing two deuterium nuclei (2.0141 u each) into helium-4 releases 23.8 MeV. (a) Calculate the number of deuterium nuclei in 1.0 kg. (b) Calculate the energy released when 1.0 kg of deuterium is fused, in units of 10¹⁴ J. Take 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. Take 1 MeV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹³ J.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.695', '10¹⁴ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) N = 1.0 ÷ (2.0141 × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷) = 2.991 × 10²⁶. (b) Reactions = N ÷ 2; E = 2.991 × 10²⁶ ÷ 2 × 23.8 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹³ = 5.69 × 10¹⁴ J. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: number of nuclei. M1: two nuclei per reaction. C1: MeV to J. C1: value. A1: E = 5.69 × 10¹⁴ J.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('aebf15a4-1de9-5594-bcd0-535be87fe33d', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', 'c7d1948a-561e-56c0-8776-090012819a77', 'a-level', '29.4', '9702 coursebook 29.4 · Mass defect and binding energy', 46, 46, 'Oxygen-16 has a binding energy of 127.6 MeV and nitrogen-15 a binding energy of 115.5 MeV. (a) Explain why removing a proton from oxygen-16 leaves nitrogen-15. (b) Calculate the energy needed to remove one proton from oxygen-16. (c) Compare it with the average binding energy per nucleon of oxygen-16. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '12.1', 'MeV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Z falls from 8 to 7 and A from 16 to 15. (b) E = 127.6 − 115.5 = 12.1 MeV. (c) It is larger than the average, 127.6 ÷ 16 = 7.98 MeV. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: Z and A change. M1: difference of binding energies. C1: value. C1: average. B1: comparison. A1: E = 12.1 MeV.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('f541d475-d439-5d28-add6-354ef96da8a0', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', 'c7d1948a-561e-56c0-8776-090012819a77', 'a-level', '29.4', '9702 coursebook 29.4 · Mass defect and binding energy', 47, 47, 'Iron-56 has a binding energy of 492 MeV and uranium-238 one of 1802 MeV. (a) Calculate the binding energy per nucleon of each. (b) Calculate (BE per nucleon of iron) − (BE per nucleon of uranium).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.214', 'MeV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Iron: 492 ÷ 56 = 8.786 MeV; uranium: 1802 ÷ 238 = 7.571 MeV. (b) 8.786 − 7.571 = 1.21 MeV. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: iron. C1: uranium. M1: difference. B1: iron more stable. A1: Δ(BE/A) = 1.21 MeV.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg74ba4c5c-t fg74ba4c5c-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg74ba4c5c-t">Iron and uranium</title><desc id="fg74ba4c5c-d">Binding energy per nucleon curve with iron-56 near the peak and uranium-238 at the heavy end marked.</desc><line x1="29.4" y1="34" x2="29.4" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="104" y1="34" x2="104" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="178.5" y1="34" x2="178.5" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="253" y1="34" x2="253" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="327.5" y1="34" x2="327.5" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="252" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-lin